('node-8-2', 'phase-08-lesson-02-back-of-the-envelope-estimation-numbers-e', 'module-9', 2, 'Lesson 9.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2120.0, 100.0, '# Lesson 8.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0, Lesson 8.1
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.2.1` Latency numbers to memorize: L1 cache (1ns), RAM (100ns), SSD read (100µs), HDD seek (10ms), cross-datacenter RTT (150ms)
  - `8.2.2` Throughput calculations: converting Monthly Active Users (MAU) and Daily Active Users (DAU) to Read/Write QPS
  - `8.2.3` Peak multiplier heuristics: average QPS vs peak QPS (typically $2\times$ to $5\times$ average load)
  - `8.2.4` Storage volumetric estimation: bytes per record $\times$ records per day $\times$ retention years $\times$ replication factor
  - `8.2.5` Bandwidth volumetric estimation: ingress bandwidth (write QPS $\times$ payload size) vs egress bandwidth (read QPS $\times$ payload size)
  - `8.2.6` Memory and cache sizing: 80/20 Pareto rule (20% of hot data generates 80% of read traffic) memory provisioning
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Confusing bits (Gbps) with bytes (GB/s); failing to account for peak load spikes leading to 50% infrastructure under-provisioning.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) with zero calculator aids.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: Automated capacity planning spreadsheet.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.2", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Calculate exact QPS, storage (5-year), RAM cache, and bandwidth requirements for Twitter (500M DAU) with zero calculator aids.", "subtopics": ["8.2.1 Latency numbers to memorize: L1 cache (1ns), RAM (100ns), SSD read (100\u00b5s), HDD seek (10ms), cross-datacenter RTT (150ms)", "8.2.2 Throughput calculations: converting Monthly Active Users (MAU) and Daily Active Users (DAU) to Read/Write QPS", "8.2.3 Peak multiplier heuristics: average QPS vs peak QPS (typically $2\\times$ to $5\\times$ average load)", "8.2.4 Storage volumetric estimation: bytes per record $\\times$ records per day $\\times$ retention years $\\times$ replication factor", "8.2.5 Bandwidth volumetric estimation: ingress bandwidth (write QPS $\\times$ payload size) vs egress bandwidth (read QPS $\\times$ payload size)", "8.2.6 Memory and cache sizing: 80/20 Pareto rule (20% of hot data generates 80% of read traffic) memory provisioning"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Confusing bits (Gbps) with bytes (GB/s); failing to account for peak load spikes leading to 50% infrastructure under-pro", "How does Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation: Numbers Every Engineer Must Know scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-3', 'phase-08-lesson-03-data-storage-paradigms-database-selection', 'module-9', 3, 'Lesson 9.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 7 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2000.0, 150.0, '# Lesson 8.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Phase 7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.3.1` Relational (RDBMS) vs NoSQL: ACID guarantees vs horizontal scalability, schema flexibility, and query patterns
  - `8.3.2` Document stores (MongoDB): nested denormalized hierarchical documents, index structures, and write-heavy workloads
  - `8.3.3` Wide-column stores (Cassandra, ScyllaDB): log-structured merge trees (LSM), tunable consistency, high write throughput
  - `8.3.4` Key-Value stores (Redis, DynamoDB): lightning sub-millisecond lookups, TTL expirations, and partition keys
  - `8.3.5` Graph databases (Neo4j): vertex and edge traversal, index-free adjacency, and social graph fraud detection
  - `8.3.6` Time-series databases (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB): columnar compression, downsampling, and high-frequency sensor/metric ingestion
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Choosing NoSQL for financial balances without transactional multi-record atomicity; choosing RDBMS for petabyte append-only logs.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis across 8 operational criteria.
- **Project Application**: System Design Portfolio: Database architectural selection guide.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 8 // Lesson 8.3: Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "8.3", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Draft a comprehensive technical decision matrix comparing PostgreSQL, Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis across 8 operational criteria.", "subtopics": ["8.3.1 Relational (RDBMS) vs NoSQL: ACID guarantees vs horizontal scalability, schema flexibility, and query patterns", "8.3.2 Document stores (MongoDB): nested denormalized hierarchical documents, index structures, and write-heavy workloads", "8.3.3 Wide-column stores (Cassandra, ScyllaDB): log-structured merge trees (LSM), tunable consistency, high write throughput", "8.3.4 Key-Value stores (Redis, DynamoDB): lightning sub-millisecond lookups, TTL expirations, and partition keys", "8.3.5 Graph databases (Neo4j): vertex and edge traversal, index-free adjacency, and social graph fraud detection", "8.3.6 Time-series databases (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB): columnar compression, downsampling, and high-frequency sensor/metric ingestion"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Choosing NoSQL for financial balances without transactional multi-record atomicity; choosing RDBMS for petabyte append-o", "How does Data Storage Paradigms & Database Selection Matrix scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-8-4', 'phase-08-lesson-04-caching-architectures-invalidation-thunde', 'module-9', 4, 'Lesson 9.4: Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 260, 9, 2060.0, 200.0, '# Lesson 8.4: Caching Architectures: Invalidation, Thundering Herd & Topologies

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Lesson 8.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `8.4.1` Caching topologies: In-memory local cache vs Distributed cache clusters (Redis, Memcached) vs Multi-tier caching
  - `8.4.2` Cache access patterns: Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading), Read-Through, Write-Through, and Write-Behind (Write-Back)
  - `8.4.3` Cache eviction policies: Least Recently Used (LRU), Least Frequently Used (LFU), First In First Out (FIFO)
  - `8.4.4` Cache Invalidation problems: cache stampede / thundering herd mitigation using mutex locking, probabilistic early expiration (XFetch)
  - `8.4.5` Cache Penetration (queries for non-existent keys): mitigating via null caching and Bloom filters
  - `8.4.6` Cache Breakdown (hot key expiry) and Cache Avalanche (simultaneous massive key expirations) mitigation with TTL jitter
